sentences.l:
But when indeed the third day fine-plaited Dawn brought to its end,
then thereupon the wind ceased, and a calm
came to be, windless;

“Ah me, since land unhoped-for he has granted me to see,
Zeus, and this gulf, having cloven through, I have crossed,
a way out nowhere appears, from the grey sea, to the outside;

And that, into the river flowing-with-the-sea, he let go,
and back a great wave carried it down the stream, and at once then Ino
received it with her own dear hands;

If, on the one hand, by the river a troublous night I keep watch through,
lest me, together, both harmful frost and soft dew,
out of my faintness, overpower my spent-breath spirit;

These, then, neither of winds did the wet force blow through, of them blowing,
nor ever did the shining sun strike with its rays,
nor did rain pass clean through;

for of leaves there was a heap, exceedingly great,
so much as either two or three men to shelter
in the wintry season, even if very much it should rage.
